{% endif %}

## Workflow

Reusable agent skills are available under `.agents/skills/` for orientation,
implementation, verification, review, documentation updates, ADRs, conventional
commits, and learning capture. `.codex/` contains thin Codex adapters that
delegate to those canonical skills.

{% if governance == "managed" %}
Use one active task at a time and load context in tiers instead of reading
project documentation twice:

```bash
make agent-status
make agent-context TASK=<id>
make agent-context TASK=<id> SKILL=<skill>
make agent-context TASK=<id> SKILL=<skill> MODE=resume
make task-start TASK=<id>
make task-review TASK=<id>
make validate-agent-skills
make validate-project
make validate-docs
```

`agent-context` is bounded by the context-map `budget` and reports included and
omitted files. Use `MODE=resume` when resuming or fixing an existing PR.

{% if workflow_mode == "pr" %}
A1 and A2 work is completed by a human GitHub merge of the task's pull request:
agents move work to review and open one pull request per task, and must never
approve or merge their own governed work. `make task-approve` is for A2
pre-start approval only in `pr` mode; no post-merge lifecycle command or
cleanup pull request is required.
{% else %}
A1 and A2 approvals are human gates recorded with `make task-approve`
(`local`/`branch` offline fallback). Agents may prepare work and move it to
review, but must not approve it.
{% endif %}
Managed lifecycle skills live under
`.agents/managed/skills/` and compose the reusable core skills where useful.
{% else %}
Start with a small bootstrap, then load only what the actual operation needs:

```bash
sed -n '1,120p' project/brief.md
make validate-agent-skills
```

Directories are exploration roots, not context dumps: open specific files under
`docs/` and the source tree only when needed, then run focused checks.
{% endif %}

